This repository contains no vendored third-party source archives. The
following third-party components are downloaded at build or run time (pinned
by version and checksum where noted) and remain under their own licenses:

Container images / build inputs
- InfluxDB 2.x, Flux, InfluxDB UI, influx-cli (InfluxData)  - MIT
  infra/release-images/influxdb/Dockerfile builds influxd from source; inputs
  are fetched by sha256 (see *.provenance.json). The libflux Cargo vendor tree
  is regenerated from flux's Cargo.lock; individual crates carry their own
  licenses (mostly MIT and/or Apache-2.0).
- dasel (Tom Wright)                                         - MIT
- Rust toolchain images (rust-lang)                          - MIT / Apache-2.0
- Go toolchain images (golang)                               - BSD-3-Clause
- PostgreSQL                                                  - PostgreSQL License
- Eclipse Mosquitto                                           - EPL-2.0 / EDL-1.0
- Prometheus, Alertmanager                                    - Apache-2.0
- Python base images                                          - PSF License

Host / release tooling
- Tailscale client (installed from pkgs.tailscale.com)        - BSD-3-Clause
- Headscale (optional, companion cloud side)                  - BSD-3-Clause
- Sigstore cosign                                             - Apache-2.0
- Aqua Security Trivy                                         - Apache-2.0
- tpm2-tools                                                  - BSD-3-Clause

Python dependencies (requirements*.txt): FastAPI, Starlette, Uvicorn,
Pydantic, SQLAlchemy, asyncpg, Alembic, influxdb-client, paho-mqtt (EPL-2.0 /
EDL-1.0), pymodbus, cryptography, structlog, prometheus-client, psutil and
others - each under its own license as published on PyPI.

ops-console JavaScript dependencies (ops-console/package-lock.json): Svelte,
Vite and others - each under its own license as published on npm.

Trademarks (Modbus, Tailscale, InfluxDB, etc.) belong to their owners. Any PLC
or HMI vendor names in the documentation are used only to describe
interoperability.
